From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 13:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] winebus: Count Bluetooth DualSense input reports ourselves.

A wired DualSense increments the counter byte of its input report with
every report. In the extended Bluetooth report #0x31 the same byte stays
constant (the transport sequence lives in the high nibble of byte 1), so
both translations that start from it published a frozen counter:

- The DualSense-as-DualShock 4 spoof copied it into the DS4 frame counter
  and touch packet counter. God of War (2018) then intermittently treats a
  held button as released and pressed again over Bluetooth (#1003).
- The Bluetooth DualSense-as-USB spoof left it in place, and its basic
  report path left it at 0.

Synthesize the counter from sony_input_seq for Bluetooth reports, as the
basic Bluetooth DS4 path already does. USB reports keep the controller's
own counter.

Root cause found by ElectricWeasel in
https://github.com/GloriousEggroll/proton-ge-custom/issues/1003, verified
against their hid-recorder captures: state[6] is 1 in all 77070 Bluetooth
reports, while the high nibble of byte 1 increments on every report.
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -1395,6 +1395,7 @@
     BYTE translated[DUALSHOCK4_INPUT_REPORT_SIZE] = {0};
     const BYTE *state;
     DWORD sensor_timestamp;
+    BYTE sequence;
 
     if (!ext->desc.spoof_dualshock4) return TRUE;
 
@@ -1445,7 +1446,14 @@
     memcpy(translated + 1, state, 4);                 /* sticks */
     translated[5] = state[7];                        /* face buttons and hat */
     translated[6] = state[8];                        /* shoulder and menu buttons */
-    translated[7] = (state[9] & 0x03) | ((state[6] & 0x3f) << 2);
+    /* Over USB the DualSense counter in state[6] increments with every
+     * report. In the Bluetooth report #0x31 that byte stays constant (the
+     * transport sequence lives in the high nibble of byte 1 instead), and a
+     * frozen DS4 counter makes some games drop held buttons, so count the
+     * reports ourselves there. */
+    if (ext->desc.bus_type == BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH) sequence = ext->sony_input_seq++;
+    else sequence = state[6];
+    translated[7] = (state[9] & 0x03) | ((sequence & 0x3f) << 2);
     translated[8] = state[4];                        /* left trigger */
     translated[9] = state[5];                        /* right trigger */
 
@@ -1469,7 +1477,7 @@
     /* The packed touch point representation is shared by both generations.
      * Publish the current DS5 contacts as one DS4 touch history sample. */
     translated[33] = 1;
-    translated[34] = state[6];
+    translated[34] = sequence;
     memcpy(translated + 35, state + 32, 8);
 
 done:
@@ -1552,6 +1560,9 @@
         if ((buf[1] & 0x0f) == 0x02) return FALSE;
         memmove(buf + 1, buf + 2, DUALSENSE_INPUT_REPORT_USB_SIZE - 1);
         buf[0] = DUALSENSE_INPUT_REPORT_USB;
+        /* The counter byte is constant over Bluetooth; a wired DualSense
+         * increments it with every report. */
+        buf[7] = ext->sony_input_seq++;
         report->length = DUALSENSE_INPUT_REPORT_USB_SIZE;
         return TRUE;
     }
@@ -1569,6 +1580,7 @@
         memcpy(buf + 1, basic + 1, 4);
         buf[5] = basic[8];
         buf[6] = basic[9];
+        buf[7] = ext->sony_input_seq++;
         memcpy(buf + 8, basic + 5, 3);
         /* Mark both touch points as inactive. */
         buf[33] = 0x80;
--
